item.text:
{
  "concepts": [
    {
      "conditions": [],
      "definition": "The frames-and-context family F1 concerns finite actors declaring, sizing, binding, activating, relating, auditing and revising bounded reference frames. REFERENCE_FRAME owns engagement law in urn:stdo:bounded-context:reference-frame-evaluation; ACTIVATION owns context construction and FOUNDATIONS owns the referenced frame objects, authority kinds, independence distinctions and frame tuple. The foundation-to-law table traces rules and choices without owning those rules.",
      "exceptions": [],
      "grounding": [
        {
          "end_line":135,
          "quote":"This family, frames and context (F1 in the index of\n[FOUNDATIONS §3 Foundation to law](FOUNDATIONS.md#foundation-to-law)),\ndecides how a finite actor engages a body of work through bounded reference\nframes: how a frame is declared, sized, bound, activated, related to other\nframes, used to audit coverage and revised.",
          "section_id":"repo://stdo-v3-generation/claude/9aee01ffa76cfbde1a6b8f1153a25bd6d71c216e/source/standards/REFERENCE_FRAME.md#REFERENCE_FRAME%2F1.%20The%20frame%20as%20the%20engagement%20unit%2FFoundation%20to%20law",
          "start_line":125
        }
      ],
      "key":"frames_and_context",
      "label":"Frames and context"
    },
    {
      "conditions": [],
      "definition":"A reference frame is a finite specification of the attention, coordinates, authority, evidence and result relations required by one evaluation family. It is a relation specification, not a required schema or runtime carrier. Its engagement atomicity means activation and evaluation as one bounded attention unit; it does not imply semantic irreducibility.",
      "exceptions": [],
      "grounding": [
        {
          "end_line":157,
          "quote":"a reference frame is a finite specification of the attention, coordinates, authority, evidence and result relations one evaluation family requires.",
          "section_id":"repo://stdo-v3-generation/claude/9aee01ffa76cfbde1a6b8f1153a25bd6d71c216e/source/standards/REFERENCE_FRAME.md#REFERENCE_FRAME%2F1.%20The%20frame%20as%20the%20engagement%20unit%2FFoundation%20to%20law",
          "start_line":157
        },
        {
          "end_line":465,
          "quote":"A reference frame is atomic at engagement altitude: it is activated and\nevaluated as one bounded attention unit. Semantic irreducibility is a\nseparate question: one frame may contain several semantic atoms, and one\nsemantic atom may take part in several frames",
          "section_id":"repo://stdo-v3-generation/claude/9aee01ffa76cfbde1a6b8f1153a25bd6d71c216e/source/standards/REFERENCE_FRAME.md#REFERENCE_FRAME%2F3.%20Laws%2FEngagement%20atom%20and%20semantic%20atom",
          "start_line":462
        }
      ],
      "key":"reference_frame",
      "label":"Reference frame"
    },
    {
      "conditions": [],
      "definition":"The questions or predicates a frame declares it supports at its declared coordinates, together with its closed result algebra. This declaration supplies the boundary against which sufficiency can be tested.",
      "exceptions": [],
      "grounding": [
        {
          "end_line":285,
          "quote":"Every frame declares the evaluation family it supports. A frame with no\ndeclared evaluation family has no testable sufficiency boundary",
          "section_id":"repo://stdo-v3-generation/claude/9aee01ffa76cfbde1a6b8f1153a25bd6d71c216e/source/standards/REFERENCE_FRAME.md#REFERENCE_FRAME%2F3.%20Laws%2FRF-001%20Evaluation%20relativity",
          "start_line":283
        }
      ],
      "key":"evaluation_family",
      "label":"Evaluation family"
    },
    {
      "conditions": [],
      "definition":"A selected actor's declared limits and capabilities, including competence, context capacity, access and tools. An activated attention surface must fit this envelope; adequate attention in actual use is a separate evidential question.",
      "exceptions": [],
      "grounding": [
        {
          "end_line":299,
          "quote":"Whether the actor attends adequately to a frame that fits is a separate\nquestion, answered by actual-use evidence under\n[EVALUATION §7](EVALUATION.md#frame-qualification); a size measurement\ndoes not answer it",
          "section_id":"repo://stdo-v3-generation/claude/9aee01ffa76cfbde1a6b8f1153a25bd6d71c216e/source/standards/REFERENCE_FRAME.md#REFERENCE_FRAME%2F3.%20Laws%2FRF-002%20Finite%20attention",
          "start_line":296
        }
      ],
      "key":"capability_envelope",
      "label":"Capability envelope"
    },
    {
      "conditions": [],
      "definition":"Authority admitted from its existing owner and scope. Frame-set, semantic, evaluation, operation and decision authority remain distinguishable, with actor identity apart; frame-set authority is human authority. An authority proxy remains distinct from the human authority.",
      "exceptions": [],
      "grounding": [
        {
          "end_line":163,
          "quote":"the division into frame-set, semantic, evaluation, operation and decision authority, with actor identity apart, and frame-set authority as human authority: FOUNDATIONS §2 Authority kinds",
          "section_id":"repo://stdo-v3-generation/claude/9aee01ffa76cfbde1a6b8f1153a25bd6d71c216e/source/standards/REFERENCE_FRAME.md#REFERENCE_FRAME%2F1.%20The%20frame%20as%20the%20engagement%20unit%2FFoundation%20to%20law",
          "start_line":163
        }
      ],
      "key":"admitted_authority",
      "label":"Admitted authority"
    },
    {
      "conditions": [],
      "definition":"A semantic atom is a domain meaning unit whose irreducibility is distinct from engagement atomicity. A domain's semantic-contraction law, where supplied by an adopting method, governs that domain atom family.",
      "exceptions": [],
      "grounding": [
        {
          "end_line":467,
          "quote":"Where an adopting\nmethod supplies a semantic-contraction law for its domain atoms, that law\ngoverns the domain atom family.",
          "section_id":"repo://stdo-v3-generation/claude/9aee01ffa76cfbde1a6b8f1153a25bd6d71c216e/source/standards/REFERENCE_FRAME.md#REFERENCE_FRAME%2F3.%20Laws%2FEngagement%20atom%20and%20semantic%20atom",
          "start_line":465
        }
      ],
      "key":"semantic_atom",
      "label":"Semantic atom"
    },
    {
      "conditions": [],
      "definition":"Human-declared project method structure comprising selected frames, evaluation inventory, capability envelopes, overlaps, checks, dependencies, authorities, exclusions, residual uncertainty and revision conditions for a governed workspace and outcome. It need not be a partition, tree, stack or exhaustive ontology.",
      "exceptions": [],
      "grounding": [
        {
          "end_line":480,
          "quote":"Human authority may declare an initial project frame set from accumulated\ndevelopment practice, known failure modes, domain expertise, the governed\noutcome's risk and knowledge of the available agents and other actors.",
          "section_id":"repo://stdo-v3-generation/claude/9aee01ffa76cfbde1a6b8f1153a25bd6d71c216e/source/standards/REFERENCE_FRAME.md#REFERENCE_FRAME%2F4.%20Frame-set%20declaration",
          "start_line":476
        },
        {
          "end_line":176,
          "quote":"a frame set need not be a partition, tree, stack or exhaustive ontology",
          "section_id":"repo://stdo-v3-generation/claude/9aee01ffa76cfbde1a6b8f1153a25bd6d71c216e/source/standards/REFERENCE_FRAME.md#REFERENCE_FRAME%2F1.%20The%20frame%20as%20the%20engagement%20unit%2FFoundation%20to%20law",
          "start_line":176
        }
      ],
      "key":"frame_set",
      "label":"Frame set"
    },
    {
      "conditions":["An evaluation was performed."],
      "definition":"One closed result in the declared core algebra or a lawful refinement mapped into it. The five named core values are satisfied, falsified, indeterminate, out_of_frame and invalid_basis; FOUNDATIONS §4 owns their meanings. A nonperformed evaluation has no evaluation result.",
      "exceptions": [],
      "grounding": [
        {
          "end_line":407,
          "quote":"Every performed evaluation of a frame returns one closed result in the\nframe's declared result algebra: the core algebra of\n[FOUNDATIONS §4 Results](FOUNDATIONS.md#results), or a refinement mapped\ninto it under that section. That section owns the five core values\n(`satisfied`, `falsified`, `indeterminate`, `out_of_frame` and\n`invalid_basis`)",
          "section_id":"repo://stdo-v3-generation/claude/9aee01ffa76cfbde1a6b8f1153a25bd6d71c216e/source/standards/REFERENCE_FRAME.md#REFERENCE_FRAME%2F3.%20Laws%2FRF-012%20Closed%20results",
          "start_line":398
        }
      ],
      "key":"closed_result",
      "label":"Closed evaluation result"
    },
    {
      "conditions": [],
      "definition":"Seven relations distinct from the frame: declare(human_authority, workspace, outcome, practices, capabilities) → frame_set; activate(frame, evaluation, subject, basis, actor) → activated_frame or activation_refusal; evaluate(activated_frame, admissible_evidence) → result plus evidence plus residuals; conjoin(decision_rule, frame_results) → decision, conflict or indeterminate; translate(source_frame, target_frame, value_or_evidence, mapping) → translated_value, declared_loss or refusal; audit_coverage(workspace, outcome, evaluation_inventory, frame_set) → coverage_ledger; revise(frame_set, new_evidence, failures, capability_change, topology_change) → proposed_frame_set_delta. These signatures describe relations rather than callable software interfaces; subsequent text supplements the signatures' outcome lists.",
      "exceptions": [],
      "grounding": [
        {
          "end_line":552,
          "quote":"declare(human_authority, workspace, outcome, practices, capabilities)\n  -> frame_set\n\nactivate(frame, evaluation, subject, basis, actor)\n  -> activated_frame | activation_refusal\n\nevaluate(activated_frame, admissible_evidence)\n  -> result + evidence + residuals\n\nconjoin(decision_rule, frame_results)\n  -> decision | conflict | indeterminate\n\ntranslate(source_frame, target_frame, value_or_evidence, mapping)\n  -> translated_value | declared_loss | refusal\n\naudit_coverage(workspace, outcome, evaluation_inventory, frame_set)\n  -> coverage_ledger\n\nrevise(frame_set, new_evidence, failures, capability_change, topology_change)\n  -> proposed_frame_set_delta",
          "section_id":"repo://stdo-v3-generation/claude/9aee01ffa76cfbde1a6b8f1153a25bd6d71c216e/source/standards/REFERENCE_FRAME.md#REFERENCE_FRAME%2F5.%20Method%20functions",
          "start_line":533
        }
      ],
      "key":"method_functions",
      "label":"Method functions"
    },
    {
      "conditions": [],
      "definition":"A declared decision rule consumes closed results of several frames using the conjunction and core values owned by FOUNDATIONS §4. REFERENCE_FRAME defines the frame relation and outcome refinement, not another conjunction.",
      "exceptions": [],
      "grounding": [
        {
          "end_line":632,
          "quote":"A declared decision rule consumes the closed results of several frames.\nThe rule, what it must state, and the conjunction it computes are\n[FOUNDATIONS §4 Conjunction of results](FOUNDATIONS.md#conjunction-of-results),\nover the core values of [FOUNDATIONS §4 Results](FOUNDATIONS.md#results);\nthis member defines no conjunction of its own.",
          "section_id":"repo://stdo-v3-generation/claude/9aee01ffa76cfbde1a6b8f1153a25bd6d71c216e/source/standards/REFERENCE_FRAME.md#REFERENCE_FRAME%2F6.%20Frames%20in%20conjunction%2FResult%20conjunction",
          "start_line":627
        }
      ],
      "key":"result_conjunction",
      "label":"Result conjunction"
    },
    {
      "conditions": [],
      "definition":"One frame supplies a value or evidence consumed inside another through a declared translation. Its fields are source and target coordinates, preserved meaning, accepted loss, provenance, equality law and refusal conditions.",
      "exceptions": [],
      "grounding": [
        {
          "end_line":639,
          "quote":"One frame supplies evidence or a value consumed inside another. The\ntranslation declares source and target coordinates, preserved meaning,\naccepted loss, provenance, equality law and refusal conditions",
          "section_id":"repo://stdo-v3-generation/claude/9aee01ffa76cfbde1a6b8f1153a25bd6d71c216e/source/standards/REFERENCE_FRAME.md#REFERENCE_FRAME%2F6.%20Frames%20in%20conjunction%2FEvidence%20or%20coordinate%20translation",
          "start_line":636
        }
      ],
      "key":"frame_translation",
      "label":"Evidence or coordinate translation"
    },
    {
      "conditions":["The evaluation requires composition."],
      "definition":"Several frame-governed operations form one material execution or reasoning relation, declared by the owning application.",
      "exceptions": [],
      "grounding": [
        {
          "end_line":654,
          "quote":"Several frame-governed operations form one material execution or reasoning\nrelation. The owning application declares the composition",
          "section_id":"repo://stdo-v3-generation/claude/9aee01ffa76cfbde1a6b8f1153a25bd6d71c216e/source/standards/REFERENCE_FRAME.md#REFERENCE_FRAME%2F6.%20Frames%20in%20conjunction%2FMaterial%20composition",
          "start_line":651
        }
      ],
      "key":"material_composition",
      "label":"Material composition"
    },
    {
      "conditions": [],
      "definition":"Named frame relation categories: disjoint shares no material variable, coordinate, evidence or result dependency for the declared evaluations; overlapping shares some material variables, evidence or entities; complementary evaluations jointly inform one decision; alternative frames evaluate the same claim under declared capability or evidence conditions; refinement resolves another frame's aggregated material distinction; restriction evaluates a lawful subset of another's domain; translated moves values or evidence through a declared coordinate map; temporally successive evaluates a changed basis later; composed combines frame relations into one governed evaluation or action; governing/subordinate inherits declared constraints where real authority or decomposition exists.",
      "exceptions": [],
      "grounding": [
        {
          "end_line":678,
          "quote":"- governing and subordinate: one frame inherits declared constraints from\n  another where a real authority or decomposition relation exists.",
          "section_id":"repo://stdo-v3-generation/claude/9aee01ffa76cfbde1a6b8f1153a25bd6d71c216e/source/standards/REFERENCE_FRAME.md#REFERENCE_FRAME%2F7.%20Frame%20relations",
          "start_line":677
        }
      ],
      "key":"frame_relation_categories",
      "label":"Frame relation categories"
    },
    {
      "conditions": [],
      "definition":"Exchange of information between frames or their activated actors. Communication is distinct from functional decomposition, authority precedence, frame configuration, subject topology and evaluation dependency.",
      "exceptions": [],
      "grounding": [
        {
          "end_line":369,
          "quote":"Communication is the exchange of\ninformation between frames, or between the actors activated on them. An\nexchange by itself grants no authority",
          "section_id":"repo://stdo-v3-generation/claude/9aee01ffa76cfbde1a6b8f1153a25bd6d71c216e/source/standards/REFERENCE_FRAME.md#REFERENCE_FRAME%2F3.%20Laws%2FRF-009%20No%20assumed%20hierarchy",
          "start_line":364
        }
      ],
      "key":"frame_communication",
      "label":"Communication"
    },
    {
      "conditions": [],
      "definition":"Coverage(W, G, B, E, S, T) binds workspace, governed outcome, exact basis, known evaluation inventory, declared frame set, and claim time or immutable checkpoint.",
      "exceptions": [],
      "grounding": [
        {
          "end_line":691,
          "quote":"Coverage is evaluated against the tuple Coverage(W, G, B, E, S, T): the\nworkspace W, the governed outcome G, the exact basis B, the known\nevaluation inventory E, the declared frame set S and the time or immutable\ncheckpoint T of the claim.",
          "section_id":"repo://stdo-v3-generation/claude/9aee01ffa76cfbde1a6b8f1153a25bd6d71c216e/source/standards/REFERENCE_FRAME.md#REFERENCE_FRAME%2F8.%20Workspace%20coverage%2FCoverage%20basis",
          "start_line":688
        }
      ],
      "key":"coverage_tuple",
      "label":"Coverage basis"
    },
    {
      "conditions": [],
      "definition":"The known material evaluations derived by the coverage evaluator from every material available source, with provenance and materiality recorded.",
      "exceptions": [],
      "grounding": [
        {
          "end_line":700,
          "quote":"The coverage evaluator derives the known evaluation inventory from every\nmaterial available source",
          "section_id":"repo://stdo-v3-generation/claude/9aee01ffa76cfbde1a6b8f1153a25bd6d71c216e/source/standards/REFERENCE_FRAME.md#REFERENCE_FRAME%2F8.%20Workspace%20coverage%2FEvaluation%20inventory",
          "start_line":699
        }
      ],
      "key":"evaluation_inventory",
      "label":"Evaluation inventory"
    },
    {
      "conditions": [],
      "definition":"Cross-cutting audit lenses, typically outcome/capability, identity/authority, lifecycle/temporal behaviour, topology/interaction seams, transport/representation, evidence/proof, change/failure/recovery/rollback, historical regression, and actor capability/context fit. A lens becomes a frame only when separately instantiated as a bounded evaluation family.",
      "exceptions": [],
      "grounding": [
        {
          "end_line":724,
          "quote":"These are lenses (FOUNDATIONS §2): they become frames only when separately\ninstantiated as bounded evaluation families",
          "section_id":"repo://stdo-v3-generation/claude/9aee01ffa76cfbde1a6b8f1153a25bd6d71c216e/source/standards/REFERENCE_FRAME.md#REFERENCE_FRAME%2F8.%20Workspace%20coverage%2FCoverage%20dimensions",
          "start_line":722
        }
      ],
      "key":"coverage_dimensions",
      "label":"Coverage dimensions"
    },
    {
      "conditions": [],
      "definition":"Per-evaluation audit record whose status answers whether, on the declared coverage tuple, a capable selected frame covers the evaluation without unresolved frame/result/authority conflict, or a cited governing authority excludes it. Baseline statuses are covered, separately_covered, conditionally_covered, conflicted, uncovered, unknown and excluded_by_authority.",
      "exceptions": [],
      "grounding": [
        {
          "end_line":757,
          "quote":"They answer\nwhether a row's coverage obligation is met, for the predicate \"on the\ndeclared coverage tuple, a capable frame of S covers the evaluation with\nno unresolved conflict among the frames, results or authorities that\ncover it, or a governing authority the row cites excludes it\".",
          "section_id":"repo://stdo-v3-generation/claude/9aee01ffa76cfbde1a6b8f1153a25bd6d71c216e/source/standards/REFERENCE_FRAME.md#REFERENCE_FRAME%2F8.%20Workspace%20coverage%2FCoverage%20ledger",
          "start_line":751
        }
      ],
      "key":"coverage_ledger",
      "label":"Coverage ledger"
    },
    {
      "conditions": [],
      "definition":"Ordered examination of activation, exact subject/basis, actor capability, admissible evidence, missing material variables/invariants, missing conjunction/translation, evaluation-family adequacy and only then the need for another frame.",
      "exceptions": [],
      "grounding": [
        {
          "end_line":891,
          "quote":"1. Was the correct declared frame activated?\n2. Were the subject and basis exact?\n3. Was the actor inside the capability envelope?\n4. Was admissible evidence available?\n5. Is a material variable or invariant missing from the frame?\n6. Is a required conjunction or translation missing?\n7. Is the evaluation family itself wrong or incomplete?\n8. Is another frame required?",
          "section_id":"repo://stdo-v3-generation/claude/9aee01ffa76cfbde1a6b8f1153a25bd6d71c216e/source/standards/REFERENCE_FRAME.md#REFERENCE_FRAME%2F10.%20Discovery%20and%20revision%2FDiagnostic%20order",
          "start_line":884
        }
      ],
      "key":"frame_diagnosis",
      "label":"Frame diagnosis"
    },
    {
      "conditions": [],
      "definition":"Carrier-neutral packet for one activated frame or declared active frame configuration. The minimal binding template identifies the activation and resolves the frame, exact evaluation, subject, basis, attention, actor, authorities, evidence, inter-frame relations, result return and stop/change conditions.",
      "exceptions": [],
      "grounding": [
        {
          "end_line":970,
          "quote":"An actor receives one activated frame or one declared active frame\nconfiguration, never an implied demand to reconstruct the complete\nworkspace from ambient context",
          "section_id":"repo://stdo-v3-generation/claude/9aee01ffa76cfbde1a6b8f1153a25bd6d71c216e/source/standards/REFERENCE_FRAME.md#REFERENCE_FRAME%2F11.%20The%20activation%20packet",
          "start_line":967
        },
        {
          "end_line":997,
          "quote":"The following carrier-neutral content is sufficient to bind one declared\nframe for one evaluation.",
          "section_id":"repo://stdo-v3-generation/claude/9aee01ffa76cfbde1a6b8f1153a25bd6d71c216e/source/standards/REFERENCE_FRAME.md#REFERENCE_FRAME%2F11.%20The%20activation%20packet%2FMinimal%20activation%20binding",
          "start_line":994
        }
      ],
      "key":"activation_packet",
      "label":"Activation packet and binding template"
    },
    {
      "conditions": [],
      "definition":"Three carrier-neutral document shapes supplying the least content to declare a frame, frame set and evaluation result. They are source templates, not populated declarations or executed evaluations.",
      "exceptions": [],
      "grounding": [
        {
          "end_line":1076,
          "quote":"The three carrier-neutral shapes below are the least content that declares\na frame, a frame set and an evaluation result. Each is sufficient as\nwritten in Markdown or any other consumer-selected carrier",
          "section_id":"repo://stdo-v3-generation/claude/9aee01ffa76cfbde1a6b8f1153a25bd6d71c216e/source/standards/REFERENCE_FRAME.md#REFERENCE_FRAME%2F13.%20Minimal%20declarations",
          "start_line":1073
        }
      ],
      "key":"minimal_declaration_templates",
      "label":"Minimal declaration templates"
    },
    {
      "conditions": [],
      "definition":"A historical mapping from received rc.2 REFERENCE_FRAME_METHOD anchors to dispositions and successor loci; unqualified loci refer to REFERENCE_FRAME. Rows received by FOUNDATIONS, ACTIVATION or EVALUATION are listed there. This section records dispositions rather than deciding substantive law.",
      "exceptions": [],
      "grounding": [
        {
          "end_line":1208,
          "quote":"RF rows received by this member. Rows that land in FOUNDATIONS, ACTIVATION\nor EVALUATION are listed in those members. Anchors are into rc.2\nREFERENCE_FRAME_METHOD.md; a locus is a section of this file unless it names\nanother member.",
          "section_id":"repo://stdo-v3-generation/claude/9aee01ffa76cfbde1a6b8f1153a25bd6d71c216e/source/standards/REFERENCE_FRAME.md#REFERENCE_FRAME%2F15.%20Source%20dispositions",
          "start_line":1205
        }
      ],
      "key":"source_disposition_register",
      "label":"Source disposition register"
    },
    {
      "conditions": [],
      "definition":"Tier-2 RELEASE owns release publication: bounded context, release identities, project-qualified references/subtrees, historical reference conservation, candidate construction, immutable RC publication, exact-cut qualification, monotonic version-line advancement, selectors, naming, scoped assets, Product subject, release-matched asset cohorts, acceptance, external qualification dependencies and successor baseline conservation STDO-UP-015 with its dispositions. Its context identity is urn:stdo:bounded-context:release-publication under the selected complete STDO basis. It owns qualification/publication/selection transitions, not Product meaning or consumer adoption.",
      "exceptions": [],
      "grounding": [
        {
          "end_line":11,
          "quote":"Tier 2. Owns release publication: the release-publication bounded\ncontext, release identities, project-qualified refs and the project\nsubtree, historical ref conservation, the three phases with candidate\nconstruction, immutable RC publication, exact-cut qualification and\nmonotonic version-line advancement",
          "section_id":"repo://stdo-v3-generation/claude/9aee01ffa76cfbde1a6b8f1153a25bd6d71c216e/source/standards/RELEASE.md#RELEASE",
          "start_line":3
        },
        {
          "end_line":42,
          "quote":"This member owns the release-publication bounded context\n`urn:stdo:bounded-context:release-publication` under the selected complete\nSTDO basis. It owns the identities and transitions used to qualify,\npublish and select released cuts. It does not own Product meaning or\nconsumer adoption",
          "section_id":"repo://stdo-v3-generation/claude/9aee01ffa76cfbde1a6b8f1153a25bd6d71c216e/source/standards/RELEASE.md#RELEASE%2F1.%20Context%20and%20position",
          "start_line":37
        }
      ],
      "key":"release_publication",
      "label":"Release publication"
    },
    {
      "conditions": [],
      "definition":"A published immutable point-in-time RC cut with product-local name v<version>-rc.<n>. Release process evaluates, bounds, names, publishes and makes the cut discoverable, distinct from live specification's current constitutional project truth.",
      "exceptions": [],
      "grounding": [
        {
          "end_line":52,
          "quote":"Release process is distinct from live project specification. Live\nspecification defines the current constitutional truth of the project.\nRelease process defines how one published point-in-time cut is evaluated,\nbounded, named, published and made discoverable",
          "section_id":"repo://stdo-v3-generation/claude/9aee01ffa76cfbde1a6b8f1153a25bd6d71c216e/source/standards/RELEASE.md#RELEASE%2F1.%20Context%20and%20position",
          "start_line":44
        }
      ],
      "key":"release_cut",
      "label":"Release cut"
    },
    {
      "conditions": [],
      "definition":"The mutable product-local v<version> selector points to the latest published immutable RC for its project and version line, where latest is the greatest published positive RC ordinal. It is a discovery aid.",
      "exceptions": [],
      "grounding": [
        {
          "end_line":71,
          "quote":"It denotes a mutable version-line selector (FOUNDATIONS §2). The selector\npoints to the latest published immutable RC cut on that line, where latest\nmeans the greatest positive RC ordinal published for that project and\nversion line.",
          "section_id":"repo://stdo-v3-generation/claude/9aee01ffa76cfbde1a6b8f1153a25bd6d71c216e/source/standards/RELEASE.md#RELEASE%2F1.%20Context%20and%20position",
          "start_line":66
        }
      ],
      "key":"version_line_selector",
      "label":"Version-line selector"
    }
  ],
  "constraints": [
    {
      "applies_to_keys":["frames_and_context"],
      "conditions":["The family governs work with the stated instance premises."],
      "exceptions":[],
